    "title": "Hypothetical RAG Sanity Check: Retrieval On vs. Off for Local LLMs",
    "body": "I'm exploring a hypothetical method to sanity-check Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) setups locally. The premise involves using a fixed set of 30 local documents.\n\nThe core idea is to compare model responses to identical questions under two conditions: with RAG retrieval enabled, and with retrieval disabled. This approach aims to isolate the impact of the retrieval component on question-answering capabilities. Importantly, this is a hypothetical scenario, and no actual performance metrics or accuracy results are available.\n\nSome of the hypothetical questions are designed to have no answer within the provided documents. This is intended to test how the system handles unanswerable queries, both with and without retrieval. The goal is to understand if retrieval helps in identifying the absence of information or if it leads to hallucinations, even in a controlled, hypothetical setup. Discussion on potential pitfalls or refinements for such a sanity check is welcome.",
