    "title": "Designing a Hypothetical RAG Validation Strategy: Retrieval vs. No Retrieval",
    "body": "This post outlines a hypothetical strategy for validating a local RAG implementation. The experiment design considers a corpus of 30 local documents.\n\nThe proposed method involves posing a set of questions to the language model in two distinct configurations: one where the RAG retrieval mechanism is active, and another where it is deactivated. The same questions would be used in both scenarios to directly compare the output. This is purely a hypothetical exercise, and no actual measurements or accuracy data have been generated.\n\nA key aspect of this hypothetical test is the inclusion of questions that intentionally lack answers within the document set. This is to observe how the model behaves when faced with queries that cannot be satisfied by the available information, under both retrieval-enabled and retrieval-disabled conditions. The objective is to gain insight into the RAG system's robustness, without any measured outcomes.",
